<h1>Running a batch</h1>
<p>A configured copy lives outside the suite repository, so it cannot import the test on its own. It
  is run through a <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/batches/">batch</a> whose items say where
  the code is: the run folder for the copy, and the suite repository for everything the copy imports
  and for the package that ships to the endpoints. A CI snapshot exported from the controller runs its
  mirrors in exactly this way.</p>
<p>The run needs the <code>orcraft</code> package installed in the Python the AI agent uses, as
  described in <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/getting-started/#install-from-pypi">Getting
  started</a>. The controller GUI is not started.</p>

<h2 id="batches">Batch file</h2>
<p>The batch file sits in the run folder beside the configured copies:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('from orcraft import (
	OrcraftBatchItem,
	OrcraftConfigField,
	OrcraftTestBatch,
	OrcraftTestBatchConfig,
)


class TcpTrafficBatchConfig(OrcraftTestBatchConfig):
	name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="TCP Traffic Fan-out")
	items = OrcraftConfigField.Items(default_factory=lambda: [
		OrcraftBatchItem(
			name="Test: TCP Transmit",
			kind="test",
			source_path="run_tcp_tx.py",
			class_name="TestTcpTx",
			import_roots=(".", "../orcraft-test-suite-network"),
			package_source_path="../orcraft-test-suite-network",
		),
		OrcraftBatchItem(
			name="Test: TCP Receive",
			kind="test",
			source_path="run_tcp_rx.py",
			class_name="TestTcpRx",
			import_roots=(".", "../orcraft-test-suite-network"),
			package_source_path="../orcraft-test-suite-network",
		),
	])


class TcpTrafficBatch(OrcraftTestBatch):
	def __init__(self) -> None:
		super().__init__(config=TcpTrafficBatchConfig())


if __name__ == "__main__":
	TcpTrafficBatch().run()') }}

<p>Relative paths in an item are relative to the batch file.</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>source_path</code> names the configured copy to run.</li>
  <li><code>import_roots</code> puts the run folder and the repository on the import path of the item's
    process, so the copy finds the test it subclasses.</li>
  <li><code>package_source_path</code> names the repository as the package uploaded to the endpoints,
    so they receive the suite and not the run folder.</li>
</ul>
<p>The batch is run from the run folder like any framework file. Its log is written to a
  <code>Logs</code> folder there.</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("python tcp_traffic_batch.py") }}

<h2 id="exit-code">Exit code</h2>
<p><code>run()</code> executes the whole batch and returns its result. The result's
  <code>status</code> is an <code>OrcraftStatus</code>. A file that has to give an exit code to a calling
  script maps it itself:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('import sys

from orcraft import OrcraftStatus


def main() -> None:
	result = TcpTrafficBatch().run()
	sys.exit(0 if result.status is OrcraftStatus.PASS else 1)') }}

<h2 id="remote-endpoints">Remote endpoints</h2>
<p>A run that reaches a remote endpoint needs the same security material as the controller: the
  authorization token and the certificates. The framework reads the token from the
  <code>ORCRAFT_CONTROLLER_AUTH_TOKEN</code> environment variable, and the certificates from the
  machine's Orcraft profile folder, where the controller's Security tab generated them. On a machine
  where the controller has completed the
  <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/endpoints/#secure-endpoint-connection">security setup</a>, the
  AI agent only needs the variable defined in the environment it runs in.</p>
